==========================================================================
 (2b) the separation as the coupling falls to its physical value
==========================================================================
     N=6, Gamma=0.3t, phi/phi0=1/4, lR1=0.12t, lEO scaled with lR2
     silicene: lambda_SO ~ 3.9 meV against t ~ 1.6 eV, i.e. ~2.4e-3 t
     polarisation taken where both models transmit >= 10% of the peak

      lR2 (t)     peak T     max|dT|      max|dPz|     dPz/lR2
      1.50e-01    1.9603     1.6030e+00   1.0131e+00      6.75
      5.00e-02    1.9943     6.4031e-01   6.3729e-01     12.75
      5.00e-03    1.9998     3.5986e-02   7.7402e-02     15.48
      2.40e-03    2.0000     1.4091e-02   3.7189e-02     15.50
      1.00e-03    1.9992     5.1043e-03   1.5494e-02     15.49
      1.00e-04    1.9980     4.5953e-04   1.5490e-03     15.49

     dPz/lR2 settles at 15.5 once lR2 <= 5e-3 t and holds to three
     digits from there down, so the separation is linear across the
     whole physical range; above 1e-2 t it saturates instead.
     At the physical coupling the effect is of order one percent
     rather than of order one, but it does not vanish, and its size
     follows from the slope rather than from the parameter point.
